Title: SEVENTEEN MINUTES
Credit: Written by
Author: GREENLIGHT test fixture
Draft date: 2026-09-04
Notes: Version 2 du scénario de test, destinée à la démonstration du mode diff. Le bar et l'hôpital ont été renommés, le numéro de téléphone corrigé, une scène ajoutée. Le reste est inchangé : ces entités doivent voir leur verdict repris sans nouvelle recherche.

FADE IN:

INT. THE PAPER LANTERN - NIGHT

Low ceilings. A neon sign buzzes behind the bar. DANIEL REYES, 34, sits alone with an untouched beer.

A man slides onto the stool beside him. MARCUS WEBB, 50s, still in surgical scrubs under his coat.

MARCUS
You're late.

DANIEL
I'm early. You're just impatient.

Marcus sets a padded envelope on the bar. Slides it over. Daniel doesn't touch it.

MARCUS
Forty. Same as last month.

DANIEL
It was thirty last month.

MARCUS
Prices went up. Ask the FDA.

Daniel finally takes the envelope. Slips it inside his jacket.

DANIEL
If anyone traces this back to the hospital —

MARCUS
Nobody traces anything. I sign the charts.

INT. SAINT ODILE MEDICAL CENTER - EMERGENCY ROOM - CONTINUOUS

Fluorescent hum. A CLERK works a triage desk under a wall clock.

Marcus pushes through the double doors, coat off, badge already clipped.

A NURSE intercepts him with a chart.

NURSE
Bay four's asking for you. Again.

MARCUS
Tell bay four I'm a doctor, not a vending machine.

He takes the chart without slowing.

EXT. HOSPITAL PARKING STRUCTURE - LEVEL 3 - NIGHT

Daniel leans against a grey sedan, plate 7XKD429. He dials a number on a burner.

DANIEL (V.O.)
It's done. He took it.

The line clicks. Daniel checks the screen: 555-0147.

He dials a second number from memory: 312-555-0188. It rings once. He hangs up. Wrong one.

INT. DANIEL'S APARTMENT - NIGHT

Bare walls. A laptop open on a folding table. Above the desk, a print of Nighthawks, curling at one corner.

Daniel logs into a webmail client. The address bar reads dreyes@example.com.

He types one line, deletes it, types it again.

ON SCREEN
"Seventeen minutes. That's all it took."

A Coca-Cola can sweats on the windowsill beside him.

INT. THE PAPER LANTERN - LATER

The bar is empty now. A radio behind the counter plays "Sweet Child O' Mine", tinny and low.

The BARTENDER wipes down the counter, watching the door.

Daniel enters. Sits in the same seat. Same untouched beer.

BARTENDER
He's not coming back, you know.

DANIEL
He always comes back.

BARTENDER
Not after tonight.

She slides a folded copy of the Chicago Tribune across the bar. The front page carries a forged
byline Daniel planted himself, paid for in cash. He reads his own lie and his face changes.

EXT. 4400 NORTH BROADWAY - DAWN

Daniel stands across the street from a shuttered storefront, hands in his pockets.

A CPD cruiser rolls past without slowing.

He waits until it turns the corner. Then he crosses.

INT. RIVERTON COUNTY COURTHOUSE - DAY

Daniel sits on a bench outside courtroom 4B, envelope on his knees.

A CLERK calls a name that isn't his. He doesn't move.

CLERK
Last call.

He stands. Walks the other way.

FADE OUT.
